\section{Resolving a member}{

Each member is \code{id} (\code{project}, \code{name}, \code{kind}, \code{version}), its optional
\code{tags}, and \code{fetch}:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{x <- datom_get_set(conn, "study001-adam")
dm <- x$members[[1]]$fetch(conn)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\code{fetch} resolves whatever the pointer points at: a table member yields data, a
set member yields another \code{datom_set}. \strong{A link pins the version it was read
at} -- it is a citation, not a subscription, so it never drifts to the latest.
Pass a connection scoped to the member's own project; same-project members
resolve through the connection you already have.

Two reads of the same set are \strong{not} \code{identical()}, because closures compare
by environment. Compare \code{m[c("id", "tags")]} instead, or use
\code{identical(a, b, ignore.environment = TRUE)}.
